# Keep the build context small and free of secrets.
#
# This governs the *backend* image, whose context is the repository root because it
# carries database/migrations as well as backend/. The frontend image has its own
# context (frontend/) and its own frontend/.dockerignore.
#
# node_modules is the big one: shipping the host's copy into the build would
# both bloat the context and risk platform-specific binaries that do not match
# the build image.

.git
.gitignore
**/.git

**/node_modules
**/dist
**/build
**/.vite
**/storybook-static

__pycache__
**/__pycache__
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info
.eggs
.tox
.venv
venv
.mypy_cache
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache

# Secrets and local state must never enter an image layer -- a layer is
# readable by anyone who can pull it, even if a later layer deletes the file.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
credentials.json
service-account*.json

*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
data/
knowledge/
generations.jsonl
catalog.json

.idea
.vscode
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

*.md
!README.md
# ...except a project's curated knowledge, which is configuration rather than
# documentation. The boot-time import catalogs the tree under /app, and excluding
# these would give a container a catalog quietly missing rows the host's import
# writes -- a difference between two "identical" deployments that nothing reports.
!backend/projects/**/*.md
docs/
notebooks/
img/

# Tests are not needed to run the image, and shipping them would put the test
# fixtures' credentials -- real strings, even if only ever used against a
# throwaway database -- into a layer anybody who can pull the image can read.
backend/tests/
backend/tools/
.github/
Makefile
.pre-commit-config.yaml

# The frontend is a separate image with a separate context. Sending it to the
# backend build would ship the whole UI into a layer that never reads it.
frontend/

# Screenshots and video from the e2e run: ~76 MB, tracked deliberately, and read by
# nobody at build time. The context is the repository root, so without this every
# backend build uploads all of it to the daemon before the first layer is even
# considered.
artifacts/

# The Q&A export, for the same reason: a build input to nothing.
qa.json

# Tooling configuration only, and the backend image runs no tooling.
pyproject.toml

# Query-result exports. `vanna/tools/run_sql.py` writes these into the working
# directory when a result is large enough to hand over as a file.
query_results_*.csv

